<v Teen Jury>Oh, do that, do that.
<v Teen Jury>哦，做吧，做吧。

00:44:51.260 --> 00:45:04.210
<v Teen Juror 8>Yeah, give her two.
<v Teen Juror 8>是的，给她两次。

00:45:04.210 --> 00:45:12.470
<v Scott Carrier>Most members of the jury have already been through the workshops. They know what the right answer is. They get excited as they write the comments to the defendants.
<v Scott Carrier>大多数陪审团成员已经参加过研讨会。他们知道正确答案是什么。他们在写给被告的评论时感到很兴奋。

00:45:12.470 --> 00:45:16.180
<v Teen Juror 6>Yeah, she needs to hear it. She needs to feel somewhat bad about it.
<v Teen Juror 6>是的，她需要听到这个。她需要对此感到有点糟糕。

00:45:16.180 --> 00:45:22.920
<v Teen Juror 9>OK, we feel that there will be people that lie and cheat your whole life, to you--
<v Teen Juror 9>好的，我们觉得会有人撒谎和欺骗你一辈子，对你——

00:45:22.920 --> 00:45:25.460
<v Teen Jury>But you can't beat them all up.
<v Teen Jury>但你不能把他们都打一顿。

00:45:25.460 --> 00:45:29.310
<v Teen Juror 6>You have to grow up.
<v Teen Juror 6>你必须长大。

00:45:29.310 --> 00:45:31.730
<v Teen Juror 8>Use your mind, not your muscles.
<v Teen Juror 8>动动脑子，别动你的肌肉。

00:45:31.730 --> 00:45:34.380
<v Teen Juror 9>All that's good.
<v Teen Juror 9>那些都很好。

00:45:34.380 --> 00:46:01.770
<v Scott Carrier>After they deliver the sentence, the defendant comes down off the stand, and I ask if she wants to talk to me for a little bit, but she looks at me as if she'd like to rip my throat out, like, where's my knife when I need it. She's not been shamed. In her neighborhood, fighting is an everyday thing, a way of life, a way of staying alive. Nobody understood what she was saying.
<v Scott Carrier>当他们宣判后，被告走下证人席，我问她是否想和我聊一会儿，但她看着我，好像想把我的喉咙撕开，比如，我需要刀子的时候它在哪儿。她没有蒙羞。在她的邻里，打架是家常便饭，是一种生活方式，一种生存方式。没人听懂她在说什么。

00:46:01.770 --> 00:47:05.270
<v Scott Carrier>I follow her out of the courtroom and down the hallway, to the room where she'll have her exit interview. I watch her sit down at a long wooden table, surrounded by social workers and police officers, and her body is there, but she is gone, checked out, no longer present for the proceedings. She just isn't going to listen anymore. I can go in and sit down and watch as the administrators try to see whether she understands her sentence, as they try to get through to her, but I don't.
<v Scott Carrier>我跟着她走出法庭，沿着走廊来到她进行离职面试的房间。我看着她坐在长木桌旁，被社会工作者和警察包围，她的身体在那里，但她人已经走了，心不在焉，不再出席会议。她只是不再听了。我可以进去坐下来看工作人员是否试图看她是否理解她的判决，当他们试图让她听进去时，但我没有。

00:47:05.270 --> 00:47:30.520
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Everyday Justice. Well, the impulse to confess, to admit the truth, to state your shame, is so important and so basic that, of course, people do not need a trial to make them do it. In 1980, a New Yorker named Allan Bridge set up a telephone line he called the Apology Line. People could call and confess to anything. They were recorded. And you could call and you could listen to other people's confessions recorded on the line.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，日常正义。好吧，承认的冲动，承认真相，陈述你的羞耻，是如此重要和基础，以至于人们当然不需要通过审判来让他们这样做。1980年，一位名叫艾伦·布里奇的纽约人设立了一条他称之为“道歉热线”的电话线。人们可以打电话来承认任何事情。他们被录音了。而且你可以打电话听听其他人录在电话线上的忏悔。

00:47:30.520 --> 00:47:47.270
<v Ira Glass>Over time, the whole thing turned into a little community of confession. People recorded messages responding to each other's apologies. Mr. Apology, Allan Bridge, would leave messages responding. And sometimes he would actually contact callers off the line. Here's some recordings from the line.
<v Ira Glass>随着时间的推移，整个事情变成了一个小的忏悔社区。人们录制信息回应彼此的道歉。道歉先生，艾伦·布里奇，会留下回复信息。有时他真的会在电话线外联系打来电话的人。这是来自电话线的一些录音。

00:47:47.270 --> 00:48:08.270
<v Caller 1>Yes, I'd like to apologize because I broke the town hall windows. I flooded the basement. I broke windows on the back of a store uptown. I feel pretty bad about it because I did so much vandalism. I'm only 16 years old. I did so much vandalism, if I was caught, I'd be in juvie hall for a pretty long time, I'd say.
<v Caller 1>是的，我想道歉，因为我打破了市政厅的窗户。我淹了地下室。我打破了城里一家商店后面的窗户。我感觉很糟糕，因为我做了那么多破坏行为。我才16岁。我做了那么多破坏行为，如果我被抓，我会在感化院待很长时间，我想。

00:48:08.270 --> 00:48:32.710
<v Caller 1>I'm sorry for harassing the Republican officials, making the bomb threats and the death notices. I'm sorry for the terrorism, the firebombing that the guy paid me to do to some guy's house. I'm sorry for-- ah, what else?-- the burnings in the streets of Smithtown, Long Island.
<v Caller 1>我很抱歉骚扰了共和党官员，制造了炸弹威胁和死亡通知。我很抱歉恐怖主义，那个家伙付钱让我去炸某人的房子的纵火行为。我很抱歉——啊，还有什么？——长岛史密斯镇街头的纵火。

00:48:32.710 --> 00:49:14.200
<v Caller 1>I'm sorry for the way I'm calling right now. I'm calling by way of a phony credit card. I'm sorry for harassing the teacher in school. I feel bad about it. I'd like to have a new lease on life. What else? I'm sorry for harassing a lot of people, for causing pain to my family. I felt so sad I was sick, I was sick by it. That's all I have to say. Yeah. So long.
<v Caller 1>我很抱歉我现在的拨号方式。我是通过假信用卡拨打的。我很抱歉骚扰学校里的老师。我对此感到很难过。我希望能有新的人生。还有什么？我很抱歉骚扰了很多人，给我的家人造成了痛苦。我感到如此悲伤，我病了，我为此病了。这就是我要说的全部。是的。再见。

00:49:14.200 --> 00:49:53.120
<v Caller 2>Hello, I'm Israeli. I want to apologize. I don't know if even what I did was wrong or right, but when I was in Israel six months before, I killed six Arabs at night with a gang of other Jewish settlers. And at the time, we thought we were-- I believe still, we're fighting for our homeland, to keep it from the Arabs, but perhaps now that I am here in America, I realized that maybe killing is not the right way, and I want to apologize. Thank you.
<v Caller 2>你好，我是以色列人。我想道歉。我甚至不知道我所做的是对还是错，但当我六个月前在以色列时，我晚上和一群其他犹太定居者一起杀死了六个阿拉伯人。当时我们以为我们是——我相信现在仍然是，我们是在为我们的家园而战，把它从阿拉伯人手中保住，但也许现在我来到了美国，我意识到杀戮可能不是正确的方式，我想道歉。谢谢。

00:49:53.120 --> 00:50:27.640
<v Caller 3>Yeah, I want to apologize for something. And maybe, my guess is it's too late to apologize for it, but I want to apologize for it now. My mother was bedridden for a while, and she used to get social security and welfare, and I had no job, and I had no way of making money. And when she was hungry or thirsty, I used to make her give me money to give her a drink. Like, she'd have to give me $5 for a glass of water, $10 for a sandwich.
<v Caller 3>是的，我想为某件事道歉。也许，我的猜测是现在道歉已经太晚了，但我想现在为它道歉。我母亲病卧在床一段时间，她过去经常领社会保障金和福利，我没有工作，我没有赚钱的途径。当她饿了或渴了，我过去常让她给我钱才给她喝的东西。比如，她得给我5美元买一杯水，10美元买一个三明治。

00:50:27.640 --> 00:51:01.810
<v Caller 3>And now she's passed away, and I can't say I'm sorry to her, because I know what I did was probably the most horrible thing in the world. And I'll never be able to say I'm sorry to her. And I hope I go to hell and burn there for this, because it wasn't right. And if there's somewhere that she can hear me, I just want to tell her I'm sorry. Thank you.
<v Caller 3>现在她去世了，我不能对她说对不起，因为我知道我所做的是世界上最可怕的事情。我永远无法对她说对不起。我希望我下地狱并在那里燃烧，因为这不对。如果有什么地方她能听到我说话，我只想告诉她对不起。谢谢。

00:51:01.810 --> 00:51:33.680
<v Caller 4>I've never told anyone this, except my shrink. I accidentally killed my younger sister when I was a very small child. And it's haunted me all my life, because I didn't really mean it. It was just a game to me. I was really too young to realize what I was doing. And I was putting her head inside a plastic garbage bag and putting a rubber band around her neck, just to see her face turn blue. And I guess it was a lot of fun.
<v Caller 4>除了我的精神科医生，我从来没告诉过任何人。我还是个很小的孩子时意外杀死了我的妹妹。它困扰了我一辈子，因为我并不是真的想那样做。对我来说那只是个游戏。我当时真的太小了，没有意识到我在做什么。我把她的头塞进一个塑料垃圾袋里，在她的脖子上套了一根橡皮筋，只是想看她的脸变蓝。我想这很有趣。

00:51:33.680 --> 00:52:05.750
<v Caller 4>And I didn't mean anything bad to happen, but I guess I didn't realize what would happen if I did this too long. And she suffocated. And I hid the plastic bag, and I went out of the house. My parents weren't home. And they never found out. They thought it was crib death, they never found out I did it.
<v Caller 4>我并不是想让任何坏事发生，但我猜我没有意识到如果我这样做太久会发生什么。然后她窒息了。我藏起了塑料袋，我走出了房子。我父母不在家。他们从来没发现。他们以为是婴儿猝死症，他们永远没发现是我做的。

00:52:05.750 --> 00:52:34.260
<v Caller 4>And you know, I've never been able to tell them. I think it would hurt them worse than losing her, to find out that I did it. I kind of wish my parents could hear this tape, but I guess they never will.
<v Caller 4>你知道，我从来没能告诉他们。我想如果发现是我做的，那对他们来说比失去她更痛苦。我有点希望我父母能听到这段录音，但我猜他们永远不会。

00:52:34.260 --> 00:53:44.420
<v Caller 5>Hi. I'm a runaway, and all I want to say is that I'm kind of sorry that I left. See, I'm 15, and I saw your number in the newspaper. And when I saw it, I had to call, because it's like, I mean, you walk around on the streets all day long, just looking for someone who just might say, hey, want a place to go? Come with me. There could be food and everything. And they won't ask for anything back. That's all I want. I guess I take up too much time on the tape, but I've just got to talk.
<v Caller 5>嗨。我是个离家出走的孩子，我只想说我有点后悔我离开了。你看，我15岁，我在报纸上看到了你的号码。当我看到它时，我不得不打，因为这就像，我的意思是，你整天在街上走来走去，只是在寻找某个人，也许会说，嘿，想有个地方去吗？跟我来。那里可能有食物和一切。他们不会要求任何回报。这就是我想要的一切。我想我在录音带上花太多时间了，但我必须得说。

00:53:44.420 --> 00:54:04.010
<v Ira Glass>The Apology Line doesn't exist anymore. Mr. Apology, Allan Bridge, died in August of 1995. When he did, the line was deluged with hundreds of messages from its regular callers, grieving. His wife Marissa says it was never clear what the Apology Line had meant to people. For years, people had used it to talk about all sorts of experiences.
<v Ira Glass>道歉热线已经不存在了。道歉先生，艾伦·布里奇，死于1995年8月。当他去世时，电话线被数百条来自其常客的哀悼信息淹没了。他的妻子玛丽莎说，道歉热线对人们意味着什么从来都不清楚。多年来，人们一直用它来谈论各种各样的经历。

00:54:04.010 --> 00:54:25.940
<v Marissa Bridge>I think that maybe the word apology means something bigger than just saying you're sorry. Or at least, maybe not the word, but the line came to mean to people a place where one could go and bring their feelings, to confess, not necessarily about doing something wrong, but to confess about having a feeling.
<v Marissa Bridge>我认为道歉这个词可能意味着比说对不起更大的东西。或者至少，也许不是这个词，但这条线对人们来说意味着一个可以去并带上他们的感受，忏悔的地方，不一定是因为做了什么错事，而是忏悔有一种感受。

00:54:25.940 --> 00:54:30.550
<v Ira Glass>When he was alive, would you ever call the line, or be tempted to call the line?
<v Ira Glass>当他活着的时候，你会打电话或者想打那个电话吗？

00:54:30.550 --> 00:54:36.680
<v Marissa Bridge>I called the line in the beginning. When I first knew him, before I moved in with him, I called the line from time to time.
<v Marissa Bridge>我在一开始打过电话。当我刚认识他时，在我搬去和他住之前，我不时地打过。

00:54:36.680 --> 00:54:38.740
<v Ira Glass>And you'd apologize for stuff?
<v Ira Glass>你会为事情道歉吗？

00:54:38.740 --> 00:54:49.700
<v Marissa Bridge>Well, yeah, sometimes, but more to get his attention than anything else. So he wouldn't forget about me up in Washington Heights, where I was living.
<v Marissa Bridge>嗯，是的，有时，但更多的是为了引起他的注意，而不是其他。这样他就不会忘记我在华盛顿高地，我住的地方。

00:54:49.700 --> 00:54:52.920
<v Ira Glass>What would you apologize for back then? Do you remember what you would say?
<v Ira Glass>你那时会为什么道歉？你还记得你会说什么吗？

00:54:52.920 --> 00:55:05.000
<v Marissa Bridge>They were all like, you know, just to get his attention. Things like, I'm really sorry I was flirting with another guy, or something like that, all very much part of the mating game.
<v Marissa Bridge>它们都像，你知道，只是为了引起他的注意。比如，我很抱歉我和另一个家伙调情了，或者类似的事情，都非常属于求偶游戏的一部分。

00:55:05.000 --> 00:55:11.950
<v Ira Glass>And once you actually had a real relationship with him, you wouldn't call the line, and you didn't feel the need to ever?
<v Ira Glass>而一旦你真正和他建立了关系，你就不会打那个电话了，你也不觉得有任何需要？

00:55:11.950 --> 00:55:28.120
<v Marissa Bridge>No, but one thing that really struck me was after he died, I really turned into an Apology Line caller, someone I would define as Apology Line caller. And the line was over, so when I really needed the line, it wasn't there.
<v Marissa Bridge>不，但有一件事真正震撼我的是在他去世后，我真的变成了一个道歉热线的打号人，我会定义为道歉热线打号人的人。电话线已经结束了，所以当我真正需要它时，它不在那里。

00:55:28.120 --> 00:55:54.090
<v Marissa Bridge>I was just completely lost, and I would have loved to have been able to call the Apology Line and have someone like Allan on the other side, very sympathetic and understanding, to help me get through it. But unfortunately, that didn't happen. So I'm a much darker person than I was before he died, and I think that I understand the callers and the line much better than I even did before.
<v Marissa Bridge>我完全迷失了，我本来会很喜欢能够拨打道歉热线，让像艾伦这样的人在另一端，非常同情和理解，来帮助我度过难关。但不幸的是，这没有发生。所以我是一个比他去世前阴暗得多的人，而且我想我比以前更能理解那些打号人和这条线了。

00:55:54.090 --> 00:56:17.520
<v Ira Glass>He died in this scuba accident where he was hit by a jet skier, who then fled the scene and wasn't caught. Have you thought about the kinds of people who would call the line, who used to call the line all the time, who had done bad things to other people, and hurt other people, and you know, then would flee the scene and nobody would ever know? Have you thought about those jet skiers in terms of that, in terms of the people who used to call the line?
<v Ira Glass>他死于这次潜水事故，他被一个摩托艇手撞了，然后那人逃离了现场，没有被抓到。你想过那些会打电话、一直打电话、对别人做了坏事、伤害别人、你知道，然后逃离现场而没人会发现的人吗？你想过那些摩托艇手，就那些事，就那些以前打电话的人吗？

00:56:17.520 --> 00:56:39.920
<v Marissa Bridge>Yes. In fact, the person that hit him would also be a prime candidate for the Apology Line, because we think that he knew that he did something, because there were witnesses that saw the accident, and they saw someone on a jet ski hit him, they circled around once and then took off, so yeah.
<v Marissa Bridge>是的。事实上，撞他的人也会是道歉热线的主要候选人，因为我们认为他知道他做了什么，因为有目击者看到了事故，他们看到有人骑摩托艇撞了他，他们绕了一圈然后逃走了，所以是的。

00:56:39.920 --> 00:56:53.220
<v Ira Glass>But you can imagine those people actually calling the line. If they would've called the line, if the line had existed for them, what would've happened? What would Mr. Apology have said if he were around to say it?
<v Ira Glass>但你能想象那些人真的打电话吗？如果他们真的打电话了，如果这条线为他们存在，会发生什么？如果道歉先生还在，他会说什么？

00:56:53.220 --> 00:57:28.140
<v Marissa Bridge>I think he would be pretty pissed off, but he would forgive them. I think that's how he would have gone for it. I think he would have been angry that he was hit. That was a total accident, but I think that if they would have apologized for hitting him, he would have forgiven them, because I think that forgiveness was a big part of his personality. I think he really believed in the power of forgiveness, and everyone can be forgiven if they're sorry. I think that's something that he really believed in.
<v Marissa Bridge>我想他会很生气，但他会原谅他们。我认为这就是他会对待它的方式。我想他会因为被撞而生气。那是一次完全的意外，但我想如果他们为撞他而道歉，他会原谅他们的，因为我认为宽恕是他个性中很大的一部分。我认为他真的相信宽恕的力量，如果他们感到抱歉，每个人都可以被宽恕。我认为那是他真正相信的东西。

00:57:28.140 --> 00:57:45.980
<v Ira Glass>Marissa Bridge, Mrs. Apology.
<v Ira Glass>玛丽莎·布里奇，道歉夫人。

00:57:45.980 --> 00:57:53.180
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Alix Spiegel, Paul Tough, and myself, with Peter Clowney and Nancy Updike. Contributing editors Jack Hitt and Margy Rochlin.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我们的节目今天由阿利克斯·斯皮格尔、保罗·塔夫和我自己制作，还有彼得·克劳尼和南希·厄普代克。特约编辑杰克·希特和玛吉·罗克林。

00:57:53.180 --> 00:57:58.820
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[鸣谢]

00:57:58.820 --> 00:58:21.640
<v Ira Glass>For information about the Apology Line, you can call this number, 212-255-2748. If you'd like a copy of any of our programs, call us at WBEZ in Chicago. They only cost $10. The phone number, 312-832-3380, 312-832-3380. Our email address, [email protected] .
<v Ira Glass>有关道歉热线的信息，你可以拨打这个号码，212-255-2748。如果你想要我们节目的拷贝，请致电芝加哥的WBEZ。它们只要10美元。电话号码，312-832-3380，312-832-3380。我们的电子邮件地址，[email protected]。

00:58:21.640 --> 00:58:29.470
<v Ira Glass>[FUNDING CREDITS]
<v Ira Glass>[资金鸣谢]

00:58:29.470 --> 00:58:32.890
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ management oversight by Torey Malatia, who reminds you that--
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ管理监督托里·马拉蒂亚，他提醒你——

00:58:32.890 --> 00:58:34.930
<v Teen Foreman>Weed is the gateway drug.
<v Teen Foreman>大麻是入门毒品。

00:58:34.930 --> 00:58:39.860
<v Ira Glass>Sure is. I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>确实是。我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带更多《这就是美国生活》的故事回来。

00:58:39.860 --> 00:58:45.450
<v Teen Prosecutor>Now if I start talking massive crap to your face, would you hit me because I provoked you?
<v Teen Prosecutor>现在如果我开始对着你的脸说极其难听的话，你会因为我挑衅你而打我吗？

00:58:45.450 --> 00:58:50.450
<v 14-year-old Defendant>Maybe.
<v 14-year-old Defendant>可能会。
